At 04:54 PM 8/5/02 -0400, Matthew Stapleton wrote:
>Please bare with me...

Wrong list for *that* suggestion! I will, however, *bear* with you.

>I went to Linux.org and search for txt to speech processor...but alas I
>did not find one.  Can anyone tell me where I might find such an
>application.  And is this the best place to search for linux apps?  Even
>if the processor generates the "computer voice" that would be fine...just
>as long as it can also output wav or mp3.

Please understand that this is outside my areas of expertise. But I did a 
quick search of the Debian package database, and these package names (and 
short descriptions) popped up:

         emacspeak - speech output interface to Emacs
         flite - a small run-time speech synthesis engine
         eflite - flite based emacspeak speech server
         festival - speech synthesis system
                 [plus about 20 subsidiary packages for festival]
         rsynth - Text to speech program
         recite - English text speech synthesizer
         konq-speaker - text-to-speech plugins for Konqueror and Kate

(There might be a couple of others, but I couldn't tell from the short 
descriptions if they were for speech synthesis or speech recognition.)

Tpyically, these packages will come from projects that can be found using 
the usual search engines (once you have a project name to search on). Good 
luck.

As to what the best place is for these searches ... in practice, I turn to 
the Debian database, then search on Google using the names it elicits. Not 
perfect, but it usually turns up what I want. Of course, I use Debian, so 
finding apps with .deb packages available is usually part of my goal when 
searching.



